+    <section class="journal" aria-labelledby="journal-title">
+      <p class="journal-kicker">From seed to cup</p>
+      <h2 id="journal-title">Roaster's Journal</h2>
+      <article id="journal-text" class="journal-copy" aria-label="Our bean sourcing process">
+        <p>Every coffee we roast begins with a question: where did this bean come from, and who helped it grow?
+          Our sourcing process starts well before a bag arrives at the roastery. We look for coffees with a clear
+          connection to their farms, producer groups, or cooperatives, and for partners who can tell us about the
+          harvest behind each lot. A beautiful cup matters, but so does understanding the work that made it possible.</p>
+        <p>We begin with the growing season. Altitude, soil, shade, and rainfall all influence how a coffee cherry
+          develops, while careful picking helps separate ripe fruit from cherries that need more time on the tree.
+          We ask our sourcing partners about these choices and about the challenges producers face from one harvest
+          to the next. Rather than expecting the same coffee to taste identical every year, we follow the seasons
+          and make room for the character of each new crop.</p>
+        <p>After harvest, processing brings another layer of flavor. Washed coffees have their fruit removed before
+          drying and can offer a clean, bright cup. Natural coffees dry inside the cherry and can develop a fuller,
+          fruitier character. Neither method is a shortcut to quality: both depend on close attention to fermentation,
+          drying, and storage. When we evaluate a sample, we consider how those decisions show up in the cup, alongside
+          what we know about the producer and the lot's journey.</p>
+        <p>At the tasting table, we cup samples side by side, looking for sweetness, balance, clarity, and a finish
+          that makes us want another sip. We also discuss traceability and pricing with our partners. Our aim is to
+          build lasting relationships that value skilled farming and make future harvests worth investing in.
+          Responsible sourcing is ongoing work, so we keep asking questions about how coffee is purchased and what
+          information can be shared, rather than treating a label as the whole story.</p>
+        <p>Once a coffee reaches us, we taste it again and develop a roast profile that suits its natural qualities.
+          Small adjustments to heat and time help us find the balance between lively acidity, rounded sweetness,
+          and comforting depth. We check batches as we roast and revisit our approach as the beans age. The goal is
+          simple: carry the care of the people who grew, processed, and transported the coffee all the way into your
+          daily cup, with a little more of its story in every sip.</p>
+      </article>
+      <button id="summarize-journal" class="btn" type="button" disabled
+        aria-describedby="journal-status" aria-controls="journal-summary">Summarize</button>
+      <p id="journal-status" class="journal-status" role="status">Checking on-device AI availability…</p>
+      <noscript><p>Enable JavaScript to use the browser's on-device summary feature.</p></noscript>
+      <div id="journal-summary" class="journal-summary" role="region" aria-labelledby="summary-title" hidden>
+        <h3 id="summary-title">The sourcing story, at a glance</h3>
+        <ul id="journal-summary-points"></ul>
+      </div>
+    </section>
